[[:db/retract 17592186172653 :question/answer-fields 17592186171543] {:db/id 17592186172044 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f8f962dd-237f-5b45-bf8e-0b4aa69b05bf" "question-dc0a5ea1-b4e4-5d2f-b036-40e69f17a373"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172045 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a62921c3-0ad7-5f20-8061-cad8dd0b028d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172046 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6774c327-b1ba-5269-8425-5f0d3e5c0aaa" "question-a71f6e18-3665-5500-82e7-6236632d7d47"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8f962dd-237f-5b45-bf8e-0b4aa69b05bf" :question/id #uuid "f8f962dd-237f-5b45-bf8e-0b4aa69b05bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97634" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a15620d6-1225-5d85-a2c5-31b5a6b6b8cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a15620d6-1225-5d85-a2c5-31b5a6b6b8cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "a15620d6-1225-5d85-a2c5-31b5a6b6b8cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7ca214f-ff26-5f2a-9519-1e9d7f9709e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f77f7a3c-392c-5cdd-8d1c-6de447042512" "answer-b252e059-53b8-580a-a924-f51a0f5bac28" "answer-506e4e03-9474-5a31-8c4a-f1ce551d5609" "answer-f7ca214f-ff26-5f2a-9519-1e9d7f9709e5" "answer-d8aeb39e-6ebd-5d27-809b-f286f7807dbe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f77f7a3c-392c-5cdd-8d1c-6de447042512" :answer/id #uuid "f77f7a3c-392c-5cdd-8d1c-6de447042512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b252e059-53b8-580a-a924-f51a0f5bac28" :answer/id #uuid "b252e059-53b8-580a-a924-f51a0f5bac28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-506e4e03-9474-5a31-8c4a-f1ce551d5609" :answer/id #uuid "506e4e03-9474-5a31-8c4a-f1ce551d5609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7ca214f-ff26-5f2a-9519-1e9d7f9709e5" :answer/id #uuid "f7ca214f-ff26-5f2a-9519-1e9d7f9709e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8aeb39e-6ebd-5d27-809b-f286f7807dbe" :answer/id #uuid "d8aeb39e-6ebd-5d27-809b-f286f7807dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dc0a5ea1-b4e4-5d2f-b036-40e69f17a373" :question/id #uuid "dc0a5ea1-b4e4-5d2f-b036-40e69f17a373" :question/math-academy-id "q-97636" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(9,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(9,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,9i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=9.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=9$ and $\\omega =-4\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)\\,j) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb6d2d2c-d5c5-59c6-8a10-7880e9a9bc99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb6d2d2c-d5c5-59c6-8a10-7880e9a9bc99" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb6d2d2c-d5c5-59c6-8a10-7880e9a9bc99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-96a4c154-63c8-5129-8a57-787e1d065082" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf5777b7-1448-5d2a-b0b0-a3758610ba8c" "answer-4c30bb07-72a4-5565-8468-407beb723740" "answer-7099fb71-cfb9-5667-9c1d-abef4c2a8aa6" "answer-d1b8a56f-3561-5330-b6e7-4fd473165781" "answer-96a4c154-63c8-5129-8a57-787e1d065082"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf5777b7-1448-5d2a-b0b0-a3758610ba8c" :answer/id #uuid "bf5777b7-1448-5d2a-b0b0-a3758610ba8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=-9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c30bb07-72a4-5565-8468-407beb723740" :answer/id #uuid "4c30bb07-72a4-5565-8468-407beb723740" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7099fb71-cfb9-5667-9c1d-abef4c2a8aa6" :answer/id #uuid "7099fb71-cfb9-5667-9c1d-abef4c2a8aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1b8a56f-3561-5330-b6e7-4fd473165781" :answer/id #uuid "d1b8a56f-3561-5330-b6e7-4fd473165781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96a4c154-63c8-5129-8a57-787e1d065082" :answer/id #uuid "96a4c154-63c8-5129-8a57-787e1d065082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(t)=9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a62921c3-0ad7-5f20-8061-cad8dd0b028d" :question/id #uuid "a62921c3-0ad7-5f20-8061-cad8dd0b028d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97641" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}+(4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{sin}^{2}⁡4t+48{cos}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48({sin}^{2}⁡4t+{cos}^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |4|=4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bba5b4d7-4309-5292-8088-c53e4d246dcb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bba5b4d7-4309-5292-8088-c53e4d246dcb" :answer-field/id #uuid "bba5b4d7-4309-5292-8088-c53e4d246dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d93d225c-e122-533c-8881-688bb8703984" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69ab6c9f-a3a5-5ce4-8790-f06da39f2e5d" "answer-a14dcc4c-31b8-57fe-9ac6-4fcf63038a6e" "answer-d93d225c-e122-533c-8881-688bb8703984" "answer-31222f3b-2ab4-5b9e-a782-7337c19d97f8" "answer-74489a4f-0f2e-5d3d-b8ce-522969cbe4d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69ab6c9f-a3a5-5ce4-8790-f06da39f2e5d" :answer/id #uuid "69ab6c9f-a3a5-5ce4-8790-f06da39f2e5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\text{ m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a14dcc4c-31b8-57fe-9ac6-4fcf63038a6e" :answer/id #uuid "a14dcc4c-31b8-57fe-9ac6-4fcf63038a6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d93d225c-e122-533c-8881-688bb8703984" :answer/id #uuid "d93d225c-e122-533c-8881-688bb8703984" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31222f3b-2ab4-5b9e-a782-7337c19d97f8" :answer/id #uuid "31222f3b-2ab4-5b9e-a782-7337c19d97f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\text{ m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74489a4f-0f2e-5d3d-b8ce-522969cbe4d4" :answer/id #uuid "74489a4f-0f2e-5d3d-b8ce-522969cbe4d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172653 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=2$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,j \\\\  & =-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-18cb798f-48e6-5767-b1da-f2a745b148f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-18cb798f-48e6-5767-b1da-f2a745b148f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "18cb798f-48e6-5767-b1da-f2a745b148f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d9f2696-3096-581e-be2a-958e487b42b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f79c33c-3cd0-568c-bb05-a2c622afa98b" "answer-c19a7ca8-149b-5715-af08-bbca65fbcc05" "answer-6d9f2696-3096-581e-be2a-958e487b42b1" "answer-7e5b72a4-dae1-5b76-9b55-33e0ea99b81d" "answer-93205ff0-67cf-54ef-8742-39a6959494ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f79c33c-3cd0-568c-bb05-a2c622afa98b" :answer/id #uuid "1f79c33c-3cd0-568c-bb05-a2c622afa98b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c19a7ca8-149b-5715-af08-bbca65fbcc05" :answer/id #uuid "c19a7ca8-149b-5715-af08-bbca65fbcc05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\,i-8\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d9f2696-3096-581e-be2a-958e487b42b1" :answer/id #uuid "6d9f2696-3096-581e-be2a-958e487b42b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e5b72a4-dae1-5b76-9b55-33e0ea99b81d" :answer/id #uuid "7e5b72a4-dae1-5b76-9b55-33e0ea99b81d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93205ff0-67cf-54ef-8742-39a6959494ba" :answer/id #uuid "93205ff0-67cf-54ef-8742-39a6959494ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,i+4\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6774c327-b1ba-5269-8425-5f0d3e5c0aaa" :question/id #uuid "6774c327-b1ba-5269-8425-5f0d3e5c0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97669" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)^{2}+(-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡5t+{50}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =5\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={5}^{2}\\cdot 2=50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3ce185f1-3680-5849-be4c-996d545fcfb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ce185f1-3680-5849-be4c-996d545fcfb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce185f1-3680-5849-be4c-996d545fcfb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f56becbb-9dcc-5d4f-a4dc-2fba708f2c28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-924e4d02-78b5-5671-b9e0-e9d2328dc287" "answer-0c31bcf8-56dc-552c-82e1-a8f67c126734" "answer-93fbaf52-d067-5773-b619-d86ddfbfd800" "answer-f56becbb-9dcc-5d4f-a4dc-2fba708f2c28" "answer-4eb2dfc5-f3e1-5fac-9360-84971d459fb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-924e4d02-78b5-5671-b9e0-e9d2328dc287" :answer/id #uuid "924e4d02-78b5-5671-b9e0-e9d2328dc287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c31bcf8-56dc-552c-82e1-a8f67c126734" :answer/id #uuid "0c31bcf8-56dc-552c-82e1-a8f67c126734" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93fbaf52-d067-5773-b619-d86ddfbfd800" :answer/id #uuid "93fbaf52-d067-5773-b619-d86ddfbfd800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f56becbb-9dcc-5d4f-a4dc-2fba708f2c28" :answer/id #uuid "f56becbb-9dcc-5d4f-a4dc-2fba708f2c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4eb2dfc5-f3e1-5fac-9360-84971d459fb6" :answer/id #uuid "4eb2dfc5-f3e1-5fac-9360-84971d459fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a71f6e18-3665-5500-82e7-6236632d7d47" :question/id #uuid "a71f6e18-3665-5500-82e7-6236632d7d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-97667" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={1}^{2}\\cdot 2=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3b486493-d623-5a70-9e04-14e7a723162e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b486493-d623-5a70-9e04-14e7a723162e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b486493-d623-5a70-9e04-14e7a723162e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d2ef61b8-9ee3-5679-99e0-7100e1e56454" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef9ccac3-25f9-53ec-a5de-8b587099fa5a" "answer-126ae55a-4a80-5260-86e9-62365c375f5e" "answer-d2ef61b8-9ee3-5679-99e0-7100e1e56454" "answer-3e7e470c-cb69-5444-a607-ccc44143b6f8" "answer-82f2b053-c9f0-50de-95d9-735df747f7c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef9ccac3-25f9-53ec-a5de-8b587099fa5a" :answer/id #uuid "ef9ccac3-25f9-53ec-a5de-8b587099fa5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-126ae55a-4a80-5260-86e9-62365c375f5e" :answer/id #uuid "126ae55a-4a80-5260-86e9-62365c375f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2ef61b8-9ee3-5679-99e0-7100e1e56454" :answer/id #uuid "d2ef61b8-9ee3-5679-99e0-7100e1e56454" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e7e470c-cb69-5444-a607-ccc44143b6f8" :answer/id #uuid "3e7e470c-cb69-5444-a607-ccc44143b6f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82f2b053-c9f0-50de-95d9-735df747f7c0" :answer/id #uuid "82f2b053-c9f0-50de-95d9-735df747f7c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173162 :tutorial/content "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173205 :tutorial/content "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\omega t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173260 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172168 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
